<h1>Vibratory Conveyors &amp; Feeders</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A vibratory conveyor moves material along a trough using controlled vibration. No belt, chain, or rollers contact the product. Electromagnetic, electromechanical, or pneumatic drives generate rapid, short-stroke oscillations that propel bulk solids in micro-hops along the conveying surface. Vibratory feeders meter material from hoppers at precise rates, while vibratory bowl feeders orient and singulate small parts for automated assembly and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>. Together, vibratory conveyors and feeders handle everything from fine powders to heavy castings across food, pharmaceutical, chemical, and metalworking industries.</p>
<h2 id="drive-heading">Vibratory Drive Types</h2>
<h3>Electromagnetic Drives</h3>
<p>Electromagnetic vibratory drives use an AC-powered electromagnet that attracts and releases a spring-loaded armature at a frequency tied to line power. <a href="https://www.eriez.com/Products/Material-Handling/Vibratory-Feeders-Conveyors/Vibratory-Screeners" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Eriez lists the electromagnetic drives on its B and C model screeners</a> at 3,600 vibrations per minute on 60 Hz power (3,000 on 50 Hz) with a 0.060 inch amplitude, and its HD screener models at a fixed 1,800 vibrations per minute. The result is high-frequency, low-amplitude vibration. Electromagnetic drives offer instant start/stop, amplitude control via voltage adjustment, and no rotating parts. They suit metering applications where feed rate must respond quickly to a controller signal.</p>
<h3>Electromechanical Drives</h3>
<p>Electromechanical vibratory drives use rotating eccentric weights on motor shafts to generate vibration. Single-shaft units produce circular vibration. Dual-shaft units with counter-rotating weights produce linear (directional) vibration. Electromechanical drives produce higher amplitudes and greater force output than electromagnetic drives, making them suitable for heavier loads and longer conveying distances. Operating frequency is set by motor speed, and is generally lower than electromagnetic drives.</p>
<h3>Natural Frequency (Tuned) Drives</h3>
<p>Natural frequency vibratory conveyors operate at the system's resonant frequency, where spring energy stores and releases with minimal input power. A small drive force sustains large oscillation amplitudes because the system resonates. Because the springs return stored energy each cycle, a tuned two-mass unit needs a smaller motor than a brute-force unit moving the same load. <a href="https://www.renold.com/media/446714/renold_brutefrcfeeders2015_v2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Renold's feeder catalog</a> describes its two-mass designs as delivering high tonnage rates with very low horsepower. They suit longer vibratory conveying runs where energy use matters. Adjusting the spring system tunes the natural frequency relative to the drive frequency.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic Drives</h3>
<p>Pneumatic vibratory drives use compressed air to power a piston or turbine that generates vibration. They suit hazardous environments where electric motors pose ignition risks (grain dust, solvent vapors, combustible powder). Pneumatic drives are also used where washdown requirements make electrical drives impractical. They require compressed air infrastructure and consume more energy than electric drives, so they serve niche applications rather than general-purpose conveying.</p>
<h2 id="equipment-heading">Vibratory Equipment Categories</h2>
<h3>Vibratory Bowl Feeders</h3>
<p>Bowl feeders orient and singulate small discrete parts for automated assembly and packaging. Parts dump into a round bowl. Vibration drives them up a helical track machined or cast inside the bowl. Tooling along the track (wiper blades, air jets, profile cutouts) rejects misoriented parts back into the bowl. Correctly oriented parts exit through a linear inline track to the downstream machine. Bowl feeders handle fasteners, caps, closures, electronic components, medical device parts, and pharmaceutical capsules.</p>
<h3>Linear Vibratory Feeders</h3>
<p>Linear feeders move material in a straight line from a hopper or bin outlet to a conveyor, scale, or process machine. An electromagnetic or electromechanical drive vibrates a trough at a controlled angle that propels material forward. Feed rate adjusts by changing vibration amplitude. Linear feeders meter powders, granules, and small parts volumetrically. Where rate accuracy matters, they are paired with a scale (loss-in-weight or belt weigh) for gravimetric control. They serve as the interface between bulk storage and downstream processes in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a>, pharmaceutical, and chemical packaging.</p>
<h3>Vibratory Conveying Troughs</h3>
<p>Vibratory troughs transport bulk materials over short to moderate distances. They replace belt conveyors where sanitation, product gentleness, or the need to perform screening/sorting during conveying justifies the higher cost. Troughs come in open and enclosed (tubular) configurations. Stainless steel troughs serve food and pharmaceutical applications. Carbon steel troughs serve mining, aggregate, and recycling.</p>
<h3>Vibratory Screeners &amp; Separators</h3>
<p>Vibratory screeners combine conveying with size separation. Material travels across a vibrating screen deck, and undersize particles fall through the mesh while oversize particles continue along the deck. Single-deck screeners make one size cut. Multi-deck screeners make multiple cuts simultaneously. Vibratory screeners serve quality control in <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject and inspection</a> operations, removing oversized, undersized, and foreign materials from bulk product streams.</p>
<h3>Vibratory Hoppers &amp; Bin Activators</h3>
<p>Vibratory hoppers and bin activators mount on storage bins and silos to promote material flow from the bin outlet. Many bulk materials (flour, sugar, powders, granules) bridge, rathole, or arch inside bins, stopping flow. Vibratory bin activators break these flow obstructions by vibrating the bin cone or outlet section. They work with vibratory feeders below the bin to create a reliable flow path from storage to process.</p>
