<h2 id="apps-heading">Vibratory Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Food processing and packaging:</strong> Vibratory conveyors distribute snack foods, candy, nuts, cereal, and frozen foods from processing to weighing and packaging machines. The gentle vibration prevents product breakage. Stainless steel troughs meet <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary requirements</a>. Vibratory distribution systems spread product evenly across multi-head combination weighers for accurate fill weights.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical tablet handling:</strong> Vibratory feeders meter tablets and capsules from bulk containers to counting machines, bottle fillers, and blister packaging lines. The gentle conveying action minimizes tablet chipping and dust generation. Enclosed vibratory tubes contain dust and prevent cross-contamination between products. Electromagnetic drives provide the precise feed rate control required for pharmaceutical accuracy.</p>
<p><strong>Parts feeding for assembly:</strong> Bowl feeders orient and singulate caps, closures, fasteners, springs, O-rings, and electronic components for automated assembly machines. Each bowl feeder is custom-tooled for the specific part geometry. A typical automated assembly machine uses multiple bowl feeders, one for each component type. <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">Cleated belt elevators</a> often feed bulk parts into bowl feeder hoppers.</p>
<p><strong>Mining and aggregate screening:</strong> Vibratory screeners classify crushed stone, sand, gravel, and mineral ore by particle size. Multi-deck vibrating screens produce multiple size fractions in a single pass. Vibratory conveyors feed material to screens and transport separated fractions to stockpiles or downstream processing.</p>
<p><strong>Recycling and waste:</strong> Vibratory conveyors and screeners sort recycled materials by size and density. Vibratory flat-deck separators remove fines (dirt, glass fragments) from mixed recyclables before manual or optical sorting. Vibratory feeders meter shredded material onto sorting conveyors at controlled rates for consistent sorting quality.</p>
<p><strong>Foundry and metalworking:</strong> Vibratory conveyors transport hot castings, forgings, and stampings from production to cooling and cleaning operations. The vibration action helps remove sand from castings (shakeout). Hot-casting service requires heat-resistant steel troughs, and the supplier should confirm the rated material temperature for the specific design.</p>
<h2 id="spec-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Material Flow Characteristics</h3>
<p>Material bulk density, particle size distribution, moisture content, and angle of repose determine vibratory conveyor sizing and drive selection. Free-flowing materials (dry sand, pellets, sugar) convey easily. Cohesive materials (wet powder, sticky granules) require higher amplitude, steeper trough angles, or anti-stick coatings. Extremely fine powders may aerosolize and require enclosed tubular designs.</p>
<h3>Trough Design</h3>
<p>Trough cross-sections include flat-bottom, round-bottom, V-bottom, and tubular. Flat-bottom troughs suit general conveying. Round-bottom troughs promote material flow and resist buildup. V-bottom troughs center material flow for discharge to a centered point. Tubular designs enclose the material completely, preventing dust emissions and contamination. Trough width and material depth determine volumetric capacity, along with the travel rate the drive produces.</p>
<h3>Isolation &amp; Mounting</h3>
<p>Vibratory equipment generates dynamic forces that transmit to the supporting structure if not properly isolated. Spring mounts (coil springs, rubber springs, or air springs) isolate the vibrating trough from the building structure. Ask the supplier for the dynamic force transmitted to the support structure, so a structural engineer can confirm the floor or mezzanine can carry it without vibration, fatigue, or noise problems. Vibratory conveyors should not be hard-mounted to mezzanines, platforms, or building steel without engineering review of the dynamic loading.</p>
<h3>Noise</h3>
<p>Vibratory equipment can be loud. Noise depends on frequency, amplitude, material type, and trough construction, so ask suppliers for measured sound levels on a comparable unit. Hard parts and metal-on-metal contact raise noise. Sound enclosures, damped trough liners, and lower-frequency drives reduce it. Under <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.95" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95</a>, an 8-hour time-weighted average of 85 dBA or more triggers a hearing conservation program, including hearing protectors provided at no cost.</p>
